cense. If you distribute a product that links to or bundles it, the whole combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0, with source available to recipients. That conflicts with shipping closed source. GPL-3.0 also has an anti-tivoization clause and a patent-license grant, which can matter if you ship hardware or hold patents. Whether it counts as distribution depends on how you ship. A hosted SaaS that users only reach over a network usually doesn't trigger it, though AGPL would. Anything you hand to customers does trigger it. That includes a desktop app, a mobile app, an on-prem install, a container image, and an SDK.\n\n  Your options are:\n  1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative.\n  2. Ask the authors for a commercial license, if they offer one.\n  3. Isolate it as a separate program that talks to your code at arm's length, such as a separate process or a network API. This is legally subtle, so get counsel to review it.\n  4. Drop the feature that needs it.\n  5. Open-source your product under the GPL.\n\n- **requests (Apache-2.0):** This is fine for closed source. You need to include the license text and any NOTICE file, and keep the copyright notices. You also need to state your changes if you modified it.\n\n- **left-pad (MIT):** This is fine. You only need to include the copyright notice and license text.\n\nBefore you ship, also check these:\n- **Transitive dependencies:** `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. `somelib` or the others may pull in more packages with their own licenses. Run a real scan, such as `pip-licenses` or `license-checker`, on the resolved tree.\n- **Third-party notices:** Ship an attribution file covering the Apache and MIT components.\n- **Version pins:** `requests==2.32` and the other pins look loose or incomplete. Pin exact versions, and confirm the license listed matches the version you actually ship, because licenses sometimes change between releases.\n- **Unverified licenses:** I'm reading the license labels from `deps.txt` and haven't checked them against the packages' own metadata. Confirm them, especially for `somelib`.\n\nI'm not a lawyer, so have counsel confirm the GPL question before release. I can also scan the repo for other places `somelib` is used if that would help.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}